   "source": [
    "feat_ref = np.vstack(feat_ref)\n",
    "gmm_ref = GaussianMixture(n_components=1, covariance_type='full', reg_covar=1e-3)\n",
    "gmm_ref.fit(feat_ref)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "76f91a84",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We then use the  symmetric Kullback-Leibler divergence to compare each component in the mixture to the reference. This allows to use the full information of the distribution and not only the mean to compare two gaussians. We tuned however the contribution of the mean term of the divergence so that it stays dominant in the selection.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "D_{KL}(\\mathcal{N}_0 \\| \\mathcal{N}_1) = \\frac{1}{2} \\Big[ \n",
    "\\underbrace{\\log \\left( \\frac{|\\Sigma_1|}{|\\Sigma_0|} \\right)}_{\\textstyle \\text{volume}} \n",
    "- \\underbrace{d}_{\\textstyle \\text{dimension}} \n",
    "+ \\underbrace{\\mathrm{Tr}(\\Sigma_1^{-1} \\Sigma_0)}_{\\textstyle \\text{covariance}} \n",
    "+ \\underbrace{(\\mu_1 - \\mu_0)^T \\Sigma_1^{-1} (\\mu_1 - \\mu_0)}_{\\textstyle \\text{mean}} \n",
    "\\Big]\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 233,
   "id": "e0c139e7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def kl_divergence_gaussians(mu0, cov0, mu1, cov1):\n",
    "    k = len(mu0)\n",
    "    cov1_inv = np.linalg.inv(cov1)\n",
    "    diff = mu1 - mu0\n",
    "\n",
    "    term1 = np.trace(cov1_inv @ cov0)\n",
    "    term2 = 5*diff.T @ cov1_inv @ diff\n",
    "    term3 = np.log(np.linalg.det(cov1) / np.linalg.det(cov0))\n",
    "    return 0.5 * (term1 + term2 - k + term3)\n",
    "\n",
    "def symmetric_kl(mu0, cov0, mu1, cov1):\n",
    "    return 0.5 * (\n",
    "        kl_divergence_gaussians(mu0, cov0, mu1, cov1) +\n",
    "        kl_divergence_gaussians(mu1, cov1, mu0, cov0)\n",
    "    )\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 234,
   "id": "af0034cf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kl_divergences = np.full(np.max(labels)+1, np.inf)\n",
    "for i in range(np.max(labels)+1):\n",
    "    if len(binned_hsv[labels==i]) > 1000:\n",
    "        kl_divergences[i] = symmetric_kl(gmm.means_[i], gmm.covariances_[i], gmm_ref.means_[0], gmm_ref.covariances_[0])\n",
    "best_label = np.argmin(kl_divergences)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f717e12",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Ideally, chocolates should belong to a single cluser, as in the example above. However, sometimes they are separated between two clusters or even more. Since it is difficult to avoid this issue, we implement a postprocessing method to merge the labels after fitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "We extract the labels in contact with the target label selected by KL divergence using binary dilation. We then use the ratio between length of contact and area of the smaller label to decide wether each touching label should be merged with the target label."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 235,
   "id": "3ddcb8cf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def merge_labels(labels, target, merge_ratio):\n",
    "    merged = labels.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "    mask = labels==target\n",
    "    boundary = binary_dilation(mask, np.ones((3,3))) ^ mask\n",
    "    touching = np.unique(labels[boundary])\n",
    "\n",
    "    for other in touching:\n",
    "        ratio = np.count_nonzero(labels[boundary]==other)/min(np.count_nonzero(mask), np.count_nonzero(labels==other))\n",
    "        if ratio > merge_ratio:\n",
    "            merged[labels==other] = target\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return merged"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 236,
   "id": "21be9a1c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "best_label = np.argmin(kl_divergences) \n",
    "merged = merge_labels(labels, best_label, 0.25)\n",
    "binary = merged==best_label"
   ]
  },
